<!--
  Rulebox App Control policy RB-ALLOW (plan 4.9, Phase 5 Step 6). Level allowlist (opt-in).

  Written by Rulebox, not copied. The structure follows the example policies Windows ships
  in C:\Windows\schemas\CodeIntegrity\ExamplePolicies (DefaultWindows_Enforced.xml): the
  well-known Microsoft root and EKU definitions are facts of the Windows signing format.

  What it allows (user mode):
    - code signed for Windows: the Microsoft Product Root 2010 / Flighting Root 2014 with the
      Windows, WHQL, HAL and ELAM EKUs, Store apps, WinRT components, DRM, anti-malware;
    - by path (App Control's runtime check refuses a path rule for any file whose folder a
      standard user can write to, so this never opens a user-writable folder):
        %WINDIR%\*, Program Files, Program Files (x86), Program Files (Arm) and the Rulebox
        folder (agent, launcher, Staff App versions\, installed apps).
  What it does NOT do: it has no kernel-mode driver rules. Kernel mode is "allow all" so that
  this policy can never stop a driver from loading (plan Section 12: a wrong App Control
  policy must not stop a PC from booting; Step 6 Do NOT: no driver rules beyond Microsoft's
  list). Recommended block rules are always deployed together with this policy (RB-BLOCKS),
  because a file must be allowed by every base policy.

  Options: unsigned policy, advanced boot options menu (the way back at start-up), UMCI,
  dynamic code security off (not enabled: it is not needed for a path and signer allow list),
  no supplemental policies. "Enabled:Audit Mode" is set or removed per variant by
  scripts/appcontrol-build.ps1; the value in this file is not the one deployed.

  VersionEx is Rulebox's own policy version: it MUST increase with every content change
  (apps/agent/src/lockdown/appcontrol.rs POLICY_VERSIONS is compared with it by a test).
-->
